            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Geopolitical Logic of the Ring Fence</h3>\n<p>Following the Battle of Buxar (1764) and the Treaty of Allahabad (1765), the East India Company's core territorial possession was the fertile Bengal Subah. Surrounded by powerful martial entities—the reviving Maratha Confederacy under Mahadji Scindia and the Afghan Rohillas—the Company was militarily incapable of conquering all of Northern India. <strong>Warren Hastings</strong> formulated a defensive strategic policy known as the <strong>Ring Fence Policy</strong>.</p>\n<p>Hastings described the policy as creating <em>'a ring of buffer states'</em> around the Company's frontiers. The essential principle was: <strong>defend your neighbor's frontier so that your own borders are never violated</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Awadh Buffer &amp; The Subsidy System</h3>\n<p>Awadh was positioned as the primary buffer state protecting Bengal from the north-west. Under the Ring Fence doctrine:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>The British pledged to defend the territorial integrity of Awadh against Marathas and Afghans.</li>\n  <li>In return, the Nawab of Awadh was forced to bear the entire financial cost of maintaining the British military contingents assigned to his defense (the <strong>Subsidy System</strong>).</li>\n  <li>If the Nawab fell into arrears, the Company annexed portions of his territory or forced him to take British loans at exorbitant interest rates.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. The Rohilla War (1774)</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Rohillas</strong>, an Afghan warrior confederacy ruled by <strong>Hafiz Rahmat Khan</strong>, occupied the territory of Rohilkhand directly bordering Awadh. Fearing a Maratha invasion in 1772, Hafiz Rahmat Khan signed a treaty with Nawab Shuja-ud-Daula of Awadh, promising to pay ₹40 lakh if the Nawab and his British allies repelled the Marathas.</p>\n<p>When the Marathas withdrew, Hafiz Rahmat Khan refused to pay the ₹40 lakh. At the <strong>Treaty of Banaras (September 1773)</strong>, Hastings agreed to loan a British brigade under Colonel Champion to Shuja-ud-Daula for ₹40 lakh. In April 1774, at the <strong>Battle of Miranpur Katra</strong>, British and Awadh troops defeated and killed Hafiz Rahmat Khan. Rohilkhand was annexed to Awadh, reinforcing the buffer wall of Bengal.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Evolution into the Subsidiary Alliance</h3>\n<p>While the Ring Fence policy was initially conceived as a <em>reciprocal, defensive measure</em> for the Company's survival, it evolved into an offensive mechanism. Lord Wellesley later stripped away the defensive pretext, turning Hastings' protective ring fence into the aggressive <strong>Subsidiary Alliance system</strong>, converting sovereign Indian princes into subservient tributaries.</p>\n",
