            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Concept and Historical Evolution</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Subsidiary Alliance System</strong> was an imperial device through which the East India Company subjugated Indian princely states without incurring direct administrative responsibilities. Although <strong>Joseph François Dupleix</strong> had first originated the practice of lending European troops for cash, and Warren Hastings had utilized it in Awadh, <strong>Lord Wellesley (1798–1805)</strong> systematized it into a comprehensive imperial doctrine.</p>\n<p>Wellesley evolved the system through four progressive stages:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>First Stage:</strong> The Company offered a friendly military detachment to assist an Indian prince in a specific campaign.</li>\n  <li><strong>Second Stage:</strong> The Company agreed to field an army in common cause with the native prince.</li>\n  <li><strong>Third Stage:</strong> The Company stationed a permanent subsidiary force within the prince's borders, for which the prince paid a regular cash subsidy.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fourth Stage (Wellesley's Innovation):</strong> The prince was required to cede sovereign territory in perpetuity whose annual revenue equaled the upkeep of the subsidiary army.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>2. Mandatory Conditions of the Subsidiary Treaty</h3>\n<p>Any Indian ruler signing a Subsidiary Alliance was compelled to accept five non-negotiable stipulations:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Surrender of External Sovereignty:</strong> The ruler surrendered all foreign relations. He could not declare war, sign peace, or negotiate treaties with any other state without the explicit consent of the British.</li>\n  <li><strong>Expulsion of European Rivals:</strong> The ruler had to dismiss all French, Dutch, or non-British European officers, drillmasters, and merchants from his service, eliminating French influence.</li>\n  <li><strong>Maintenance of British Subsidiary Force:</strong> A permanent contingent of British-officered troops was stationed within the state's territory. The ruler was obligated to fund this force either through cash subsidies or by ceding fertile districts in full sovereignty.</li>\n  <li><strong>The British Resident:</strong> The ruler accepted a <strong>British Resident</strong> at his capital, who ostensibly served as a diplomatic envoy but in practice became the paramount dictator of internal state governance.</li>\n  <li><strong>British Guarantee:</strong> The Company pledged to defend the ruler from all external invasions and internal rebellions.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Strategic Advantages for the British</h3>\n<p>The Subsidiary Alliance was an extraordinary geopolitical triumph for the East India Company:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Imperial Army at Native Expense:</strong> The Company maintained massive, combat-ready armies stationed at strategic choke points across India entirely at the expense of Indian taxpayers.</li>\n  <li><strong>Forward Defense:</strong> In any war, the fighting occurred in the territory of allied states, completely sparing British provinces from devastation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Exclusion of European Rivals:</strong> Permanently shut the door on French Napoleonic ambitions in India.</li>\n  <li><strong>Disarmament of Princes:</strong> Indian states were compelled to disband their own national armies, preventing military modernization.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>4. Ruinous Consequences for Indian Princely States</h3>\n<p>For Indian states, the alliance acted as a fatal gilded trap:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Loss of Independence:</strong> Rulers lost all national sovereignty and self-respect.</li>\n  <li><strong>Economic Ruin:</strong> Exorbitant subsidiary payments bankrupted state treasuries, resulting in peasant distress.</li>\n  <li><strong>Moral Decay &amp; Misgovernance:</strong> Protected from internal rebellion by British bayonets, princes had no incentive to govern well. They became tyrannical, decadent, and neglectful, which the British subsequently used as grounds for annexation (as in Awadh, 1856).</li>\n</ul>\n",
