            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Chronological Triumphs of the Subsidiary Alliance</h3>\n<p>Between 1798 and 1805, Lord Wellesley executed the Subsidiary Alliance with relentless imperial drive, drawing the major states of India into the British orbit:</p>\n\n<h3>2. Detailed Examination of Allied States</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Hyderabad (1798 &amp; 1800):</strong>\n    <p>On <strong>September 1, 1798</strong>, <strong>Nizam Ali</strong> signed the treaty. The French contingent of 14,000 disciplined troops under French general Raymond was disarmed and disbanded overnight, and six battalions of British subsidiary troops were stationed at Secunderabad. In <strong>1800</strong>, the Nizam signed a modified treaty, ceding the entire territory acquired from Mysore in 1792 and 1799 (known as the <strong>Ceded Districts</strong>: Bellary, Anantapur, Cuddapah, and Kurnool) to pay for the subsidiary force.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Mysore (1799):</strong>\n    <p>Following Tipu Sultan's death in the storming of Srirangapatna, Mysore was subjected to an oppressive subsidiary alliance. The British reserved the right to take over the direct administration of the state if revenue fell into arrears (invoked by Bentinck in 1831).</p>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Tanjore, Surat &amp; Carnatic (1799–1801):</strong>\n    <p>In October 1799, Raja Sarfoji II of <strong>Tanjore</strong> surrendered his civil and military administration to the Company for a pension. In 1800, the administration of <strong>Surat</strong> was taken over. In July 1801, Wellesley annexed the entire <strong>Carnatic</strong> on the pretext that Nawab Umdat-ul-Umara had corresponded treasonously with Tipu Sultan, exiling the Nawab's family on a pension.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Awadh (November 1801):</strong>\n    <p>Nawab <strong>Saadat Ali Khan</strong> was subjected to brutal diplomatic intimidation. When the Nawab complained of excessive military charges, Wellesley forced him to cede <strong>over half of his entire territory</strong>—including <strong>Rohilkhand, Gorakhpur, and the lower Ganga-Yamuna Doab</strong> (yielding ₹1.35 crore annually). Awadh was completely surrounded by British territory.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>The Maratha Powers (1802–1805):</strong>\n    <p>Peshwa Baji Rao II accepted the alliance via the <strong>Treaty of Bassein (1802)</strong>. Following the Second Anglo-Maratha War, Raghuji Bhonsle signed the <strong>Treaty of Deogaon (1803)</strong>, Daulat Rao Scindia signed the <strong>Treaty of Surji-Anjangaon (1803)</strong>, and Holkar submitted at <strong>Rajghat (1805)</strong>.</p>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Rajput States:</strong>\n    <p>The Rajput principalities of Jodhpur, Jaipur, Macheri (Alwar), Bharatpur, and Bundi entered subsidiary treaties to shield themselves from Maratha and Pindari plundering.</p>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n",
