            "title": "The Doctrine of Lapse (Lord Dalhousie, 1848–1856)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Philosophical &amp; Political Origins</h3>\n<p>Between 1848 and 1856, <strong>Lord Dalhousie (James Andrew Broun-Ramsay, Earl of Dalhousie)</strong> served as Governor-General. A fervent believer in British utilitarian governance, Dalhousie held that direct British rule was far superior to the 'archaic and corrupt' administration of native Indian princes. To facilitate rapid annexation, he weaponized an imperialist legal theory known as the <strong>Doctrine of Lapse</strong>.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Traditional Hindu Law of Adoption vs. Imperial Lapse</h3>\n<p>Under centuries-old <strong>Hindu customary law</strong>, a king or landholder lacking a natural male heir possessed the unquestioned religious and civil right to adopt a son (<em>Dattaka Putra</em>). The adopted son performed the father's funeral rites and inherited all personal wealth, titles, and political sovereignty.</p>\n<p>Dalhousie unilaterally subverted this custom. He asserted the feudal concept of <strong>Paramountcy</strong>: that the East India Company was the supreme feudal lord of India. Consequently, a feudatory state could not pass political sovereignty to an adopted heir without the prior sanction and approval of the paramount power. Dalhousie routinely withheld this approval, declaring that the sovereignty <strong>'lapsed'</strong> (reverted) to the British Crown.</p>\n\n<h3>3. Dalhousie's Tripartite Classification of States</h3>\n<p>Dalhousie categorized all Indian princely states into three distinct classes:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>First Class (Independent States):</strong> States that were never subordinate to the Mughal or Peshwa empires and were not created by the British (e.g., Nepal, Hyderabad). In these states, the British claimed no right to interfere with adoptions.</li>\n  <li><strong>Second Class (Tributary / Subordinate States):</strong> States that had previously owed allegiance to the Mughal Emperor or the Peshwa and now acknowledged British paramountcy. In these states, permission to adopt was discretionary and could be refused by the British.</li>\n  <li><strong>Third Class (Dependent States):</strong> States that owed their very existence to British grants, treaties, or letters of patent (e.g., Satara, Mysore). In these states, <strong>the right of adoption was strictly prohibited</strong>, and upon the death of a ruler without a biological son, the state automatically lapsed to the British.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>4. Critique and Outrage</h3>\n<p>In practice, Dalhousie applied this arbitrary distinction whenever a state had fertile soil, rich agricultural revenues, or strategic transport value. British historian <strong>Sir John Kaye</strong> observed that Dalhousie's doctrine <em>'shook the faith of the people in the justice and good faith of the British government.'</em> It struck at the core of Hindu religious sentiment, spreading existential panic among all 500+ princely dynasties and driving rulers like Rani Lakshmibai of Jhansi and Nana Saheb of Kanpur into the forefront of the Great Revolt of 1857.</p>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "The Doctrine of Lapse was formulated and aggressively implemented by Lord Dalhousie (Governor-General 1848–1856) to accelerate the direct territorial annexation of Indian princely states.",
                "Legal Basis: Under traditional Hindu law, a ruler without a male heir had the sacred right to adopt a son to inherit both private property and political sovereignty.",
                "Dalhousie's Doctrine: The paramount British power claimed that while adopted sons could inherit private personal property, they had NO legal right to inherit political sovereignty without the prior sanction of the British Crown.",
                "Classification of Indian States: (1) Independent States (exemption); (2) Dependent States created by British charter (adoption totally prohibited); (3) Tributary/Subordinate States (adoption sanctioned only as rare imperial favor).",
                "Objective: Extinguish the sovereignty of native ruling houses and replace indirect princely rule with direct British administrative annexation."
            ],
