            "title": "Charter Acts of 1833 and 1853",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Charter Act of 1833 (Saint Helena Act)</h3>\n<p>Passed during the era of Whig liberalism in Britain (the Great Reform Act of 1832) under Governor-General <strong>Lord William Bentinck</strong>, the <strong>Charter Act of 1833</strong> represented the zenith of legislative and administrative centralization:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Governor-General of India:</strong> The Governor-General of Bengal was redesignated as the <strong>Governor-General of India</strong>, vested with all civil and military powers. <strong>Lord William Bentinck</strong> became the first Governor-General of India.</li>\n  <li><strong>Legislative Centralization:</strong> The Governors of Bombay and Madras were completely stripped of their independent legislative powers. The Governor-General-in-Council was conferred exclusive legislative authority for the entire expanse of British India. Laws passed were now officially termed <strong>'Acts'</strong> (rather than 'Regulations').</li>\n  <li><strong>Complete Abolition of Commercial Activity:</strong> The Company's commercial monopoly over the <strong>tea trade</strong> and <strong>trade with China</strong> was totally terminated. The East India Company ceased to exist as a trading corporation, becoming a purely political and administrative instrument of the British Crown.</li>\n  <li><strong>Law Member &amp; Law Commission:</strong> A fourth member—the <strong>Law Member</strong>—was added to the Governor-General's Council for legislative drafting (without the right to vote on executive matters). <strong>Lord Thomas Babington Macaulay</strong> was appointed the first Law Member. A <strong>Law Commission</strong> was appointed under Macaulay to codify all Indian laws, which eventually culminated in the drafting of the <strong>Indian Penal Code (IPC)</strong> and the Codes of Civil and Criminal Procedure.</li>\n  <li><strong>Section 87 (Equality of Opportunity):</strong> A historic statutory declaration: <em>'No native of the said territories, nor any natural-born subject of His Majesty resident therein, shall, by reason only of his religion, place of birth, descent, color or any of them, be disabled from holding any place, office or employment under the said Company.'</em> While mostly a dead letter in practice, it became a potent nationalist charter for equality.</li>\n  <li><strong>Abolition of Slavery:</strong> Directed the Government of India to take steps to mitigate and abolish slavery, leading to the legal abolition of slavery under Act V of 1843.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Charter Act of 1853</h3>\n<p>Enacted during the Governor-Generalship of <strong>Lord Dalhousie</strong>, this was the final Charter Act passed by the British Parliament:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Separation of Powers:</strong> For the first time, the <strong>legislative</strong> and <strong>executive</strong> functions of the Governor-General's Council were clearly demarcated.</li>\n  <li><strong>Establishment of Central Legislative Council:</strong> Added <strong>six new legislative members</strong> to the Council, creating a 12-member body known as the <strong>Indian (Central) Legislative Council</strong>. It functioned as a miniature Parliament, adopting British parliamentary procedures (questions, committee stages, and public debates).</li>\n  <li><strong>Introduction of Open Competition for Civil Services:</strong> The patronage system of the Court of Directors was abolished. The Act introduced an <strong>open competitive examination</strong> for recruitment into the Indian Civil Services, throwing it open to all British subjects including Indians. In 1854, the <strong>Macaulay Committee (Committee on the Indian Civil Service)</strong> was appointed to frame regulations, fixing Haileybury as the training institute.</li>\n  <li><strong>Indefinite Charter:</strong> Crucially, unlike the Acts of 1793, 1813, and 1833 (which renewed the Company's authority for 20-year terms), the Act of 1853 permitted the Company to retain possession of Indian territories <em>'in trust for Her Majesty, until Parliament should otherwise direct.'</em> This paved the way for the direct transfer of power to the Crown after the 1857 Revolt.</li>\n</ol>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Charter Act of 1833: Final step towards centralized administration; Governor-General of Bengal redesignated as 'Governor-General of India' (Lord William Bentinck was the 1st GGI).",
                "Complete End of Commercial Enterprise: EIC's remaining monopolies (tea trade and trade with China) were completely abolished; Company became purely an administrative organ.",
                "Addition of Law Member: Fourth member added to Governor-General's Council for legislative purposes; Lord Macaulay appointed first Law Member; First Law Commission formed to codify Indian laws (IPC).",
                "Section 87 of 1833 Act: First non-discrimination clause stating that no native of India would be disabled from holding any office/employment under the Company based on religion, race, or caste.",
                "Charter Act of 1853: Last of the Charter Acts; separated executive and legislative functions; introduced open competitive examinations for Indian Civil Services (ICS)."
            ],
