            "title": "Warren Hastings (1773–1785 AD)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Consolidation and Revenue System</h3>\n<p><strong>Warren Hastings</strong> assumed office in 1772 as Governor of Bengal and became the first <strong>Governor-General of Bengal in 1773</strong> under the Regulating Act. Finding Bengal shattered by the 1770 famine and ruined by Clive's Dual Government, Hastings took vigorous centralizing steps:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Abolition of Dual Government (1772):</strong> Dismissed Naib Diwans Mohammad Reza Khan and Shitab Rai, shifted the central treasury to Calcutta, and created European <strong>District Collectors</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Quinquennial &amp; Annual Revenue Settlements:</strong> Introduced a 5-year revenue farming system (1772) auctioning land revenue to the highest bidder; when it proved disastrous, he replaced it with annual settlements in 1777.</li>\n  <li><strong>Financial Measures:</strong> Stopped the annual tribute of ₹26 lakh to Shah Alam II after the Emperor returned to Delhi under the escort of Maratha general Mahadji Scindia, declaring the Emperor a Maratha puppet. He sold Kora and Allahabad back to the Nawab of Awadh for ₹50 lakh.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>2. The Orientalist Renaissance</h3>\n<p>Unlike later Anglicists, Hastings was a scholar of Persian and Urdu who championed the <strong>Orientalist school of administration</strong>, believing that stability required understanding Indian languages and heritage:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Calcutta Madrasa (1781):</strong> Founded the first British state educational institution in India to teach Persian, Arabic, and Muslim jurisprudence.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Asiatic Society of Bengal (1784):</strong> Actively encouraged <strong>Sir William Jones</strong> (Judge of the Supreme Court) to establish the Asiatic Society at Calcutta to research Indian antiquities, arts, and literature.</li>\n  <li><strong>Translation of Classical Texts:</strong> Supported <strong>Charles Wilkins</strong>, who completed the <strong>first English translation of the Bhagavad Gita</strong> in 1785 (for which Hastings wrote a brilliant, appreciative preface: <em>'The Gita will survive when the British dominion in India shall have long ceased to exist.'</em>). In 1776, N.B. Halhed compiled <em>A Code of Gentoo Laws</em> (translation of the Manusmriti).</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>3. Major Controversies and High-Handed Actions</h3>\n<p>Desperate to replenish the Company's treasury drained by simultaneous wars with the Marathas and Mysore, Hastings committed egregious acts of imperial extortion:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The Chait Singh Affair (1781):</strong> Hastings demanded arbitrary, extraordinary war contributions from <strong>Raja Chait Singh of Banaras</strong>. When Chait Singh delayed paying a 5-lakh fine, Hastings marched to Varanasi, arrested the Raja, provoked an armed insurrection, and deposed Chait Singh, quadrupling the state's revenue tribute.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Begums of Awadh (1781–1782):</strong> In collusion with Nawab Asaf-ud-Daula, Hastings sent British troops to besiege the palace of the <strong>Begums of Awadh</strong> (widow and mother of Shuja-ud-Daula) at Faizabad. The royal ladies' elderly eunuch treasurers (Jawahar Ali and Bahar Ali) were imprisoned and tortured until they surrendered over £1 million (₹1 crore) in gold and family jewels to the Company.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Judicial Murder of Nand Kumar (1775):</strong> Raja Nand Kumar leveled formal charges of bribery against Hastings before the Supreme Council. Hastings and Chief Justice Elijah Impey arranged Nand Kumar's trial on trumped-up charges of forgery under an obsolete English statute, resulting in his hanging.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>4. Historic Impeachment Trial (1788–1795)</h3>\n<p>Hastings resigned and returned to England in 1785. In 1788, the House of Commons impeached him before the House of Lords. The prosecution was led by the greatest orators of the age: <strong>Edmund Burke, Charles James Fox, and Richard Brinsley Sheridan</strong>. Burke denounced Hastings as <em>'the common enemy and oppressor of all.'</em> The trial lasted seven years; Hastings was ultimately acquitted in 1795, but his personal fortune was ruined by legal expenses.</p>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Warren Hastings served as the first Governor-General of Bengal (1773–1785), steering the East India Company through profound external wars and internal financial crises.",
                "Administrative Measures: Abolished the corrupt Dual Government (1772); stopped the ₹26 lakh imperial tribute to Mughal Emperor Shah Alam II (who had sought Maratha protection); sold Kora and Allahabad to Awadh (Treaty of Banaras 1773).",
                "Cultural & Educational Patronage: Established the Calcutta Madrasa (Aliah University) in 1781 for Islamic jurisprudence; patronized Sir William Jones who founded the Asiatic Society of Bengal (1784); supported Charles Wilkins's first English translation of the Bhagavad Gita (1785).",
                "Wars Fought: First Anglo-Maratha War (1775–1782, ended with Treaty of Salbai); Second Anglo-Mysore War (1780–1784, ended with Treaty of Mangalore); Rohilla War (1774).",
                "Impeachment: Hastings was impeached by the British Parliament (1788–1795) led by Edmund Burke, Charles James Fox, and Sheridan for corruption, tyranny against Raja Chait Singh of Banaras, and extortion of the Begums of Awadh (acquitted in 1795)."
            ],
            "keyNotes": [
                "Warren Hastings was an ardent Orientalist who believed that the British must govern India in accordance with native Indian customs and ancient texts, encouraging translations of Sanskrit and Persian works.",
                "The extortion of the Begums of Awadh (confiscation of their private estates and treasure) and the brutal deposition of Raja Chait Singh of Banaras were the central scandals of his trial."
            ],
