            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Appointment &amp; Special Powers</h3>\n<p>Following Warren Hastings' turbulent tenure, the British government sought an aristocratic, incorruptible statesman of unimpeachable integrity to lead India. <strong>Lord Cornwallis (Charles Cornwallis, 1st Marquess Cornwallis)</strong>, who had commanded British forces during the American War of Independence, was appointed. Cornwallis accepted the post only after Parliament passed the <strong>Amending Act of 1786</strong>, which conferred upon him the combined powers of <strong>Governor-General and Commander-in-Chief</strong> and granted him the legal authority to <strong>override his council</strong> in critical matters.</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Permanent Settlement of Bengal (May 1, 1793)</h3>\n<p>Cornwallis's most enduring legacy was the <strong>Permanent Settlement (Istamrari Bandobast)</strong> introduced across Bengal, Bihar, and Orissa (later extended to Varanasi and Northern Circars). Devised in consultation with <strong>Sir John Shore</strong> (President of the Board of Revenue) and James Grant:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Zamindars as Proprietors:</strong> Traditional revenue collectors (Zamindars) were converted into absolute, hereditary owners of the land.</li>\n  <li><strong>Fixed Demand:</strong> State land revenue demand was fixed permanently in perpetuity at <strong>10/11th for the Company</strong> and <strong>1/11th for the Zamindar</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Sunset Law:</strong> If the zamindar failed to remit the fixed revenue by sunset on the appointed date, his estate was instantly seized and auctioned.</li>\n  <li><strong>Consequences:</strong> While it provided a stable, predictable annual revenue of ₹2.68 crore to the Company and created a loyal landed aristocracy, it completely sacrificed the rights of millions of peasant cultivators to feudal exploitation.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Administrative and Judicial Innovations: The Cornwallis Code (1793)</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>The Cornwallis Code:</strong> A body of 48 statutory regulations based on the principle of <em>Rule of Law</em> and the <em>Separation of Powers</em>. He stripped District Collectors of all judicial and magisterial authority, transferring civil justice to independent European <strong>District Judges</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Judicial Hierarchy:</strong> Reorganized courts from the Sadar Diwani Adalat at Calcutta down through 4 Provincial Courts of Appeal, District Courts, Registrar Courts, and native Munsif courts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Police Reorganization:</strong> Abolished zamindari police forces; divided districts into Thanas under Indian <strong>Darogas</strong> supervised by the District Magistrate.</li>\n  <li><strong>Civil Service Purge:</strong> Professionalized the administration by raising salaries, strictly banning private commerce and gifts, but instituted a rigid racial glass ceiling barring Indians from all senior posts.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>4. Third Anglo-Mysore War (1790–1792)</h3>\n<p>Cornwallis demonstrated decisive military leadership in the <strong>Third Anglo-Mysore War</strong>. He forged the Triple Alliance with the Marathas and Nizam, captured Bangalore, besieged Srirangapatna, and forced Tipu Sultan to surrender half his kingdom under the <strong>Treaty of Srirangapatna (1792)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cornwallis returned to England in 1793. In <strong>1805</strong>, he was reappointed Governor-General to reverse Wellesley's aggressive forward policy, but fell ill and died on <strong>October 5, 1805 at Ghazipur (Uttar Pradesh)</strong>, where a grand monument marks his tomb.</p>\n",
            "pointsToRemember": [
                "Lord Cornwallis (1786–1793) arrived in India vested with extraordinary powers: appointed both Governor-General and Commander-in-Chief with the right to override his council.",
                "The Permanent Settlement of Bengal (1793): Cornwallis's most famous agrarian reform; fixed land revenue permanently in perpetuity with zamindars recognized as absolute owners.",
                "Cornwallis Code (1793): Enacted 48 regulations separating judicial from revenue functions, instituting the Rule of Law and professionalizing the police.",
                "Purification of Civil Services: Father of the Indian Civil Service; banned private trade, raised salaries substantially, and introduced the Covenanted Civil Service.",
                "Military Leadership: Personally commanded British armies in the Third Anglo-Mysore War (1790–1792), defeating Tipu Sultan and dictating the Treaty of Srirangapatna."
            ],
