            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. Imperial Philosophy &amp; Historical Context</h3>\n<p>Arriving in India in 1798 at the age of 38, <strong>Richard Wellesley (Earl of Mornington, later Marquess Wellesley)</strong> found British dominion threatened by French global expansion (Napoleon's invasion of Egypt) and powerful, modernized regional powers (Tipu Sultan and the Marathas). Rejecting the non-interventionist policy of his predecessor Sir John Shore, Wellesley pursued an aggressive forward policy. He proudly proclaimed that his mission was to transform <em>'the British Empire in India into the British Empire of India'</em>.</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Instrument of Conquest: Subsidiary Alliance</h3>\n<p>Wellesley made the <strong>Subsidiary Alliance</strong> his primary weapon. Within seven years, he effectively subjugated or dismembered the premier states of India:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Hyderabad (1798 &amp; 1800):</strong> First state to accept; disbanded French troops under Raymond; surrendered Ceded Districts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Mysore (1799):</strong> Defeated and killed Tipu Sultan; carved up the territory and installed the Wodeyars under a subsidiary treaty.</li>\n  <li><strong>Tanjore &amp; Surat (1799–1800):</strong> Annexed administrative control.</li>\n  <li><strong>Carnatic (1801):</strong> Annexed on charges of secret correspondence with Tipu Sultan.</li>\n  <li><strong>Awadh (1801):</strong> Extorted half the kingdom (Rohilkhand and the Doab).</li>\n  <li><strong>Maratha Confederacy (1802–1803):</strong> Coerced Peshwa Baji Rao II into the Treaty of Bassein (1802), followed by crushing victories over Scindia and Bhonsle.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Cultural &amp; Administrative Initiatives</h3>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Fort William College (July 10, 1800):</strong> Founded at Calcutta to train young British civil servants in Indian languages, laws, and customs. Although the Court of Directors ordered it downgraded, it produced pivotal translations, grammars, and dictionaries in Bengali, Hindi, and Urdu.</li>\n  <li><strong>Censorship of Press Act (1799):</strong> Fearing that French agents might pass military intelligence through newspapers, Wellesley enacted the <strong>Censorship of the Press Act (1799)</strong>. It mandated that every newspaper carry the names of the printer, publisher, and editor, and submit all proofs to the Government Secretary for pre-censorship before publication.</li>\n  <li><strong>Government House at Calcutta (Raj Bhavan):</strong> Commissioned the construction of the magnificent neoclassical Government House in Calcutta (modeled on Kedleston Hall in Derbyshire), remarking that <em>'India should be governed from a palace, not from a counting-house.'</em></li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>4. The Second Anglo-Maratha War &amp; Recall (1805)</h3>\n<p>Wellesley's forward policy reached its climax in the <strong>Second Anglo-Maratha War (1803–1805)</strong>. His younger brother, <strong>Major-General Arthur Wellesley</strong>, won legendary victories at Assaye and Argaon, while <strong>General Lake</strong> took Delhi and Laswari. However, when war dragged on against <strong>Jaswant Rao Holkar</strong> (who defeated Colonel Monson at the Mukundwara Pass and besieged Delhi) and Raja Ranjit Singh of Bharatpur repulsed Lake's assaults, the Court of Directors panicked. Facing an astronomical debt of £31 million, the Directors recalled Wellesley in <strong>July 1805</strong> and sent back Lord Cornwallis with instructions to restore peace at all costs.</p>\n",
