            "title": "Lord Hastings (1813–1823 AD)",
            "summary": "",
            "theoryHtml": "\n<h3>1. The Doctrine of Paramountcy</h3>\n<p>Appointed Governor-General in 1813, <strong>Lord Hastings (the Earl of Moira, later Marquess of Hastings)</strong> reversed the non-intervention policies of George Barlow and Lord Minto I. He enunciated the doctrine of <strong>British Paramountcy</strong>: asserting that the East India Company was the supreme sovereign power of the subcontinent, and that all Indian princely states were subordinate to its paramount authority.</p>\n\n<h3>2. The Anglo-Nepal War (1814–1816) &amp; Treaty of Sagauli</h3>\n<p>The expanding Gurkha kingdom of Nepal clashed with the British over the border districts of Butwal and Sheoraj (Gorakhpur frontier):</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Military Campaigns:</strong> Initial British thrusts suffered bloody reverses against brave Gurkhas led by <strong>Amar Singh Thapa</strong> and <strong>Balbhadra Singh</strong> (at the defense of Nalapani). British fortunes were restored by <strong>General David Ochterlony</strong>, who outflanked the Gurkhas at the Makwanpur Pass, threatening Kathmandu.</li>\n  <li><strong>The Treaty of Sagauli (March 1816):</strong>\n    <ol>\n      <li>Nepal ceded the Himalayan territories of <strong>Garhwal and Kumaon</strong> in the west, and surrendered its claims to the low-lying <strong>Terai</strong>.</li>\n      <li>Nepal withdrew from Sikkim, recognizing it as a British protectorate (Mechi River fixed as eastern border).</li>\n      <li>A permanent <strong>British Resident</strong> was posted at Kathmandu.</li>\n      <li>The British acquired strategic health resorts and hill stations: <strong>Shimla, Mussoorie, Nainital, and Almora</strong>.</li>\n      <li>The British began recruiting <strong>Gurkha soldiers</strong> into the Indian Army, establishing an unbroken tradition of military service.</li>\n    </ol>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>3. Extirpation of the Pindaris &amp; Maratha Annexation (1817–1818)</h3>\n<p>Hastings assembled two massive armies (the Northern Army under himself and the Southern Army under Sir Thomas Hislop, totaling 113,000 men and 300 guns) to surround Central India. By 1818, the Pindari predatory bands were annihilated: Karim Khan surrendered and was given an estate in Gorakhpur, Wasil Muhammad committed suicide in prison, and Chitu was eaten by a tiger in the Asirgarh jungles.</p>\n<p>Simultaneously, Hastings crushed the final rebellion of the Maratha chiefs in the <strong>Third Anglo-Maratha War (1817–1818)</strong>, abolished the Peshwaship, pensioned Baji Rao II to Bithoor, and annexed Maharashtra to the Bombay Presidency.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Internal Administration &amp; Social Measures</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Land Settlements:</strong> Hastings sanctioned <strong>Sir Thomas Munro's Ryotwari Settlement in Madras (1820)</strong> and Holt Mackenzie's <strong>Mahalwari Settlement</strong> (Regulation VII of 1822) in the North-Western Provinces.</li>\n  <li><strong>Press Liberalization:</strong> In 1818, Hastings relaxed Wellesley's harsh 1799 press censorship rules, abolishing pre-censorship. This allowed the emergence of the first Indian-owned newspapers: J.S. Buckingham's <em>Calcutta Journal</em> (1818) and Raja Ram Mohan Roy's <em>Sambad Kaumudi</em> (1821).</li>\n  <li><strong>Education:</strong> Supported the founding of Hindu College (1817) and the Calcutta Book Society (1817).</li>\n</ul>\n",
